RUNNING MAY

NAME

    may - check, verify and compile May contracts

SYNOPSIS

    cargo run -- help
    cargo run -- check FILE
    cargo run -- verify FILE
    cargo run -- compile FILE
    just algorand-counter-smoke
    just algorand-counter-smoke ADDRESS

REQUIREMENTS

    Rust
    Cargo
    Z3
    Just
    AlgoKit
    Docker or OrbStack

The project pins the Rust toolchain. If rustup is installed, cargo should
pick the right compiler for you.

The verifier uses Z3 through the Rust bindings. If the build cannot find Z3,
install it with the system package manager.

On macOS:

    brew install z3

COMMANDS

    cargo run -- help

Print the CLI surface.

    cargo run -- check examples/counter.may

Parse the source and run the semantic pass.

    cargo run -- verify examples/counter.may

Parse, bind and ask the verifier to prove the must bounds and transition.

    cargo run -- compile examples/counter.may

Run the same front end and verifier first. If verification fails, compile does
not write artifacts.

    just algorand-accounts

Print the LocalNet accounts known to goal. Use one funded address as the
creator for the smoke test.

    just algorand-counter-smoke

Run the Algorand smoke test with the first funded LocalNet account.

    just algorand-counter-smoke ADDRESS

Run the same smoke test with an explicit funded creator address.

ALGORAND SMOKE TEST

The smoke test proves that the generated artifacts are accepted by the
Algorand developer tooling and can be executed against a real LocalNet.

It starts LocalNet, compiles the May source, asks goal to compile both TEAL
programs, creates the application, calls the increment fn with one integer
argument and reads global state back from the chain.

The ADDRESS argument is the application creator. It must be a funded LocalNet
account. If ADDRESS is omitted, or if the literal word ADDRESS is passed, the
recipe asks goal for the account list and chooses the first address it sees.

For example:

    just algorand-counter-smoke \
        JVSGUVZ74EMJBV3MV2S3P3O7QJXRPFNTWMEMVLS5PPS73HE64HVSDSNG5Q

The last argument is the increment amount. It defaults to 5.

    just algorand-counter-smoke \
        JVSGUVZ74EMJBV3MV2S3P3O7QJXRPFNTWMEMVLS5PPS73HE64HVSDSNG5Q \
        10

Success means three separate things happened:

    goal accepted approval.teal and clear.teal
    goal created an application with one global integer slot
    increment updated the value key in global state

The final read should include a value entry. With the default amount it should
end as:

    "value": {
        "tt": 2,
        "ui": 5
    }

If goal cannot create the application, the recipe prints goal output before it
exits. That matters because most failures are not May parser failures. They are
usually a bad creator address, an unfunded account, a stopped LocalNet or TEAL
that Algorand rejected.

FILES

Successful Algorand compilation writes target output next to the source file:

    examples/build/algorand/

That directory contains:

    approval.teal
    clear.teal
    application.json

NOTES

The generated TEAL is deliberately simple. It dispatches on the first
application argument, updates global state for the supported assignment subset
and rejects calls it does not understand.

EXIT STATUS

    0
        Success.

    non-zero
        Parse errors, semantic errors, verification failures, emission errors
        or file-system errors.
